# G080 — Scheduled Auto Login and Action

*v2. See `scheduler.md` for prose: tasks are data, catch-up policy is
skip-missed (not backfill), Schedule as sum type (not cron string),
disabled tasks are retained, per-task history is immutable, dispatch
as the extension point.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Automation

[vault-automations @dispatcher
    IN ORDER TO
        register a handful of data-driven scheduled tasks
        tick the scheduler on a fixed cadence and record every run
        so the schedule survives restart and history is queryable

    @sched <- [scheduler[new]]

    [scheduler[add-task
                {sched: @sched, name: "daily backup",
                 schedule: {kind: "daily", hour: 3, minute: 0},
                 action: "backup-vault",
                 credential: "backup-host",
                 first-fire-base-ms: [clock[now]]}]]

    [scheduler[add-task
                {sched: @sched, name: "agent heartbeat",
                 schedule: {kind: "every_ms", interval-ms: 60000},
                 action: "ping-agents", credential: none,
                 first-fire-base-ms: [clock[now]]}]]

    every 1 second
        @runs <- [scheduler[tick
                              {sched: @sched,
                               now-ms: [clock[now]],
                               dispatch: @dispatcher}]]
        for @r in @runs
            @r where status is "failed"
                -> [alert[dispatch
                           {task: @r:task-name,
                            message: @r:message}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[scheduler
    new
    add-task             {sched, name, schedule, action,
                          credential, first-fire-base-ms}
    remove-task          {sched, id}
    set-enabled          {sched, id, enabled}
    tick                 {sched, now-ms, dispatch}        ;; -> [Run]
    history-for          {sched, id}
    all-history          {sched}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: schedule as sum type, next-fire computed per variant

[scheduler[next-fire {task: @t, now-ms: @now}]
    @t:schedule
        where kind is "every_ms"
            -> @t:last-fire-ms + interval-ms
               (or @t:first-fire-base-ms if never fired)
        where kind is "at_ms"
            -> @t:schedule:absolute-time
        where kind is "daily"
            -> next occurrence of (hour, minute) at or after @now
]

Enum variants beat cron strings: trivially typecheckable, no parser,
explicit variant-per-pattern. Trades expressiveness for safety — add
a new pattern is add a new variant.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: skip-missed as policy `where` deadline passed

[scheduler[tick {sched: @s, now-ms: @now, dispatch: @d}]
    IN ORDER TO
        fire each enabled task whose next-fire is at or before @now
        once per tick, advancing its last-fire to the *current* boundary
        so a 30-missed-fire backlog collapses to a single run

    for @task in @s:tasks
        where @task:enabled
        and [scheduler[next-fire {task: @task, now-ms: @now}]] at most @now
            -> @run <- [@d {task: @task, now-ms: @now}]
            -> append @run to @s:history
            -> @task:last-fire-ms := [scheduler[most-recent-boundary
                                                  {task: @task,
                                                   at-or-before: @now}]]
               (NOT @now literally — aligns next fire to the schedule)
]

Thundering-herd-after-downtime is almost never what the caller wants.
Cron picks skip-missed; systemd's Persistent=false matches. Backfill
semantics require an explicit opt-in pattern on top.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: dispatch as injected extension

[scheduler[tick {..., dispatch: @d}]
    @d is any value callable as (task, now-ms) returning a Run
    IN ORDER TO
        keep the scheduler ignorant of what tasks actually do
        tests plug a deterministic fake @d
        production plugs a real @d that looks up credentials
                     and invokes the named action
]

Same abstract-transport pattern as G072 File Downloader. The
scheduler is reusable across any task semantic; the dispatch is where
the per-project work lives.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @s <- [scheduler[new]]
    [scheduler[add-task
                {sched: @s, name: "heartbeat",
                 schedule: {kind: "every_ms", interval-ms: 1000},
                 action: "ping", credential: none,
                 first-fire-base-ms: 0}]]

    -> [scheduler[tick
                    {sched: @s, now-ms: 500,
                     dispatch: @noop-dispatch}]]
    ;; -> []  (not yet due)

    -> [scheduler[tick
                    {sched: @s, now-ms: 1500,
                     dispatch: @noop-dispatch}]]
    ;; -> [Run{task: "heartbeat", status: "ok"}]

    -> [scheduler[tick
                    {sched: @s, now-ms: 10000,
                     dispatch: @noop-dispatch}]]
    ;; -> [Run{...}]  (ONE run, not 8 backfilled — skip-missed)

    -> [scheduler[history-for {sched: @s, id: 0}]]
    ;; -> [two Runs, chronological]
]
